WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Film Courage: How old was your dad when&nbsp;
he found out he had terminal cancer?

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.360
Steven Shea, Filmmaker: 48, I&nbsp;
think he was 48 if memory serves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.560
I think he was around 48 and he got diagnosed with&nbsp;
renal cell carcinoma which is a kidney cancer. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:25.600
removed a soft ball-sized tumor off his kidney.&nbsp;
He was a vegetarian, didn't drink, didn't smoke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.120
had a pretty healthy lifestyle but had just&nbsp;
gotten out of a really bad multi-year lawsuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.320
that caused a lot of stress which probably&nbsp;
probably helped out a lot I'm sure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.560
It was at the time they had given him eight&nbsp;
months and so we pushed up our wedding and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.600
everything. He ended up lasting eight years.&nbsp;
He was a fighter for sure. He definitely fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.320
tooth and nail and so ended up lasting&nbsp;
about eight years on it which was great.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.080
Film Courage: Where were you when you found out?

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.360
Steven: It's funny I actually remember exactly&nbsp;
where I was. I was working at the video superstore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.080
in Orlando, Florida and he called while I was&nbsp;
on a shift and I remember going out and sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.040
in the car on my lunch break I think and he&nbsp;
told me about it. I remember very vividly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.480
and it was just kind of like Oh&nbsp;
okay…you know this big hit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.200
How did you re-evaluate your lifestyle and your&nbsp;
life at that point because you're kind of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.200
oh wow okay and rich realize really quickly&nbsp;
how sacred life is for sure but I remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.240
sitting in my 88 Suburban in that parking lot&nbsp;
when he called and told us and it was like oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.320
okay and then trying to figure out what to do from&nbsp;
there and when you walked back into the store did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:54.160
the store look different in a way because your&nbsp;
perspective now everything was turned upside down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.600
I don't know if necessarily my perspective was&nbsp;
completely turned upside down it was more so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:08.160
of okay okay like what do we do next&nbsp;
what's the next step I'm definitely a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.040
structured kind of person so it's like all&nbsp;
right if I have to accomplish something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.920
what do I have to do to get there like okay&nbsp;
what's next we do surgery next okay we do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.080
surgery remove the tumor okay then once after that&nbsp;
then we start chemotherapy okay so like what's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:30.240
plan to get to our goal of remission so I think&nbsp;
my mind was kind of more thinking that way than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:39.360
thinking about oh I'm gonna go cry for an hour and&nbsp;
a half you know and be sad I think it was more so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.920
about okay here we are what do we have to do to&nbsp;
accomplish our goal to make to make this work to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:52.800
make this better which of course you only have&nbsp;
so much say in because it's it's a disease but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.400
and then they started the processes and you&nbsp;
stuck around for another eight years it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:05.360
crazy it was cool how did that day impact his life&nbsp;
and how did that day impact yours he definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:11.920
made a lot of choices and changes after that&nbsp;
he moved back to his home state of Louisiana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:19.760
he got closer to family over there he ended up&nbsp;
meeting a woman Beth who they start a relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.160
and she had a big farm and he started a farm which&nbsp;
he never had a farm before we bought him goats for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.840
Christmas I remember her sheep sheep sheep and&nbsp;
goats because that was fun and she started this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.560
farm he was a lawyer and a judge for years and&nbsp;
so he kind of took to this retirement he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:43.360
have to go to Houston to do chemotherapy and&nbsp;
through the MD Anderson Center and then after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.520
a few of those years he started traveling you know&nbsp;
trying to travel to places that he hadn't been to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.080
he tried to kind of live live the best he&nbsp;
could he got remarried during that to Beth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.560
and you know lived as much as he could possibly&nbsp;
do but it was interesting because at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.520
I have two of my sisters and I went and stayed&nbsp;
with them two weeks apart so we each had two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.040
weeks with them and I had the last two weeks&nbsp;
and I was leaving because our short film 222 was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.120
playing at Grauman's Chinese Theatre here in&nbsp;
Hollywood which was the first time i'd ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.280
had a movie play in Hollywood let alone at the&nbsp;
Grauman's Chinese Theatre it was a huge huge deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.360
and I was there with him for two weeks and then&nbsp;
I took a flight to Hollywood I met my wife she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.080
flew out from Florida we went to this whole red&nbsp;
carpet had this whole incredible experience got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.880
meetings from studios and all this different stuff&nbsp;
I put her back on a flight the next day because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.520
had set up a few days worth of meetings while I&nbsp;
was there and as soon as she got on the flight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.440
and left I remember going back and I was just&nbsp;
hanging out just looking at the Hollywood sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:48.400
soaking everything in and then I get a call saying&nbsp;
he's not going to last the next day so it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.600
really crazy experience it was like the highest&nbsp;
and lowest point I think i'd ever experienced in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:01.520
the same time frame and so I was like well okay&nbsp;
and so I went and booked a flight you know flew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:07.200
back to the small town Louisiana that he was in&nbsp;
cancelled all my meetings cancelled everything and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.960
and yeah he passed away the next day&nbsp;
and so it was a crazy whirlwind for sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.600
and what's what's even crazier is three months&nbsp;
after that my stepbrother ended up getting killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.760
by an accidental shooting and three months&nbsp;
after that my wife's mother died of cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:33.360
so it was a very crazy year and also our son was&nbsp;
born right after she passed away a few months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.360
after she passed he was born but it really going&nbsp;
back to the whole horror element it really lit a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:45.040
fire to where it's like all right you know your&nbsp;
life is not guaranteed tomorrow's not guaranteed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.440
like you've got to get going you got to make sure&nbsp;
that everything you're doing is impacting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.560
way that you want to go and to help you reach&nbsp;
this goal that you've set for yourself and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.720
you know soon after that we moved out here and&nbsp;
and kind of changed where we were doing things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.840
and it's it's been great it's been wonderful but&nbsp;
it's that fire that kind of keeps keeps pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.320
because yeah it's you never know you know and i've&nbsp;
had friends that have passed away that are younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.640
than me and stuff and even like we've had friends&nbsp;
pass away that you know just didn't get had their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.520
whole chance taken from them and so like you see&nbsp;
that and it's like oh my gosh you know and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:30.880
your monster really goes crazy because it doesn't&nbsp;
want to die either and you really kind of got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:36.560
figure out what's what's the best plan you know&nbsp;
to get what you want and where you want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.560
and once you decalculate all that I feel&nbsp;
like you you use your secret decoder ring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.760
and see how the industry works and you're kind&nbsp;
of like okay this makes sense this makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.200
this makes sense you see what connects&nbsp;
the dots it does make it a little easier

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:58.800
but it's still pretty hard when you talked about a&nbsp;
passion a passionate filmmaker versus an obsessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:06.240
filmmaker do at times you check yourself because&nbsp;
you realize wait a minute okay i've seen somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:12.000
who was who had a thriving career and and other&nbsp;
people that i've known pass away how much is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.520
this really worth obsessing over I'm definitely a&nbsp;
workaholic like I like being busy I like having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:24.480
lot of stuff going on at the same time though I&nbsp;
get frazzled also too because of it and I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:31.680
a family I have an 11 year old son so it's trying&nbsp;
to find that that work-life balance of being there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.640
for your family and then also continuing to&nbsp;
push to be able to get to where you want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.680
and it's definitely challenging at times there's&nbsp;
some I mean I just went away for I was six weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:47.200
on the road doing shoots and then had to go home&nbsp;
for some family stuff for a couple weeks and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.720
even though it was like six weeks away from home&nbsp;
is is tough and then you're trying to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.960
okay well i've got two hours a night of down&nbsp;
time that I could try to write something or try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.480
to put something else together like while I'm&nbsp;
on this trip or I'm on a plane for five hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.080
we'll kind of get done while I'm on the plane&nbsp;
which most of the time you just watch movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.080
because you're exhausted and you don't you don't&nbsp;
want to do anything but it's it's trying to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:12.160
that balance and and also still enjoying things&nbsp;
you know you don't want to work yourself to death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:19.040
to where you're 80 years old looking back being&nbsp;
like oh man I shouldn't have you know killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:23.440
myself writing those 37 scripts when I could&nbsp;
have just gone out and gone camping for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:29.520
weekend or something with my family and I think&nbsp;
it's important to find that to find that balance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.960
it's not always easy to find it but you're always&nbsp;
trying you're always trying to find it I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.600
do you ever think about when you turn 48 I know&nbsp;
you have many years to go before that happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.160
oh my gosh no no I didn't think about that till&nbsp;
now but oh my god i've got eight years you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:50.960
for anybody who's had a parent pass away it's a&nbsp;
weird thing that when you become their age when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.040
they pass away it's like this weird perspective&nbsp;
that I don't know maybe I'm just the only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.400
that thinks that no no I'm terrified no I didn't&nbsp;
even think about that until you just brought it up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:04.960
oh well no no no I mean and just in a way of you&nbsp;
just see life in an interesting way and it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:10.560
have to be tragic actually well interesting too my&nbsp;
mother was diagnosed with cancer the month after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:16.560
I was born at 20. and she had hodgkin's disease&nbsp;
luckily which is the most curable kind of cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:22.480
but the amount of chemotherapy she went through&nbsp;
in 1981 has caused a lot of damage on her body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:28.480
now later in life so now she's 61 and her heart is&nbsp;
completely falling apart she has a lot of really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:33.280
bad heart problems and her heart's way too damaged&nbsp;
for a transplant or anything else like that too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.240
so now you kind of watch that side as well and&nbsp;
you're like okay you're trying to balance you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:46.160
everything else too with that and how much time&nbsp;
you have left with them and what you can do to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:50.960
it's kind as a child you know you're always&nbsp;
trying to figure out what you can do to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:56.800
your parents proud of you right so you kind of&nbsp;
always have that in mind too that it's like okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:02.000
while she's still here like what can you do to&nbsp;
be like hey look I just did this thing that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.560
know maybe they're impressed by so you know they&nbsp;
don't know about everything so it's sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:09.760
it's hard to impress you like oh I got this thing&nbsp;
happens I don't know what that is like okay cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:16.480
but I do remember the first time my movie came&nbsp;
in a store and I called my dad my dad was always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:21.440
he was very skeptical of everything and he was&nbsp;
skeptical of like everyone's skeptical the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:24.480
industry when their kids go into it because they&nbsp;
don't understand it they don't understand how it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.360
works they understand there's lots of work and&nbsp;
you can make lots of money and there's lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:32.480
different things about it I mean it took&nbsp;
my my wife's parents forever to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.240
I don't know you know they've still figured it&nbsp;
out like what exactly we do and how we survive but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.720
I remember calling my dad and being like hey&nbsp;
the movie came out today it's like oh okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:44.560
I was like no no it's a best buy you can go&nbsp;
buy it and he's like what are you talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.840
I'm like no it's on the shelf like go&nbsp;
you can go to best buy and pick it up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.360
and he called me an hour later and he's like&nbsp;
you're not going to believe this your movie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:55.680
at best buy and I'm like I just told you that&nbsp;
like you didn't he didn't believe me I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:59.840
and he was like I just bought every copy and I'm&nbsp;
like well great I know nobody else can watch it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:04.800
but it's moments like that that you know you&nbsp;
want you want of course you want your parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:11.200
to be proud of you and and my mom's my biggest&nbsp;
cheerleader she's awesome she's I do a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:15.840
horror stuff so it's like she can't always promote&nbsp;
everything to her friends but at least now the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:23.440
documentary she can but yeah I mean you're always&nbsp;
trying to find that that approval from peers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:32.080
family and friends and everything I think do&nbsp;
you think it made you grow up faster I mean just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:39.520
I was when I grew up in high school I was the&nbsp;
oldest of seven kids my dad and mom divorced and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:46.640
remarried and I had three step siblings and a half&nbsp;
sister and so I was the oldest of seven so I felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:52.000
then that I grew up really quickly and I always&nbsp;
had more than one job like I started working at 16&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:57.520
not that I had to work I just like to work I like&nbsp;
to be busy so I was working at the movie theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:04.400
the video store the film commission and the tv&nbsp;
station all at the same time and in the keys the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:08.720
keys are 180 miles long people don't realize&nbsp;
how big they are so I was driving around four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:13.920
hours a day I drive an hour north to school&nbsp;
two hours down to key west to work and then an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.880
hour home every night which is fun in l.a because&nbsp;
someone's like oh I don't want to drive 20 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:25.760
like oh i'll drive the other side of the city it&nbsp;
doesn't bother me two hour drive sure but it's

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.960
i forget where we were going with that that's&nbsp;
funny because the people I don't wanna drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:35.520
over the hill I don't wanna go over you&nbsp;
know laurel canyon and it's like okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:40.880
you showed up on meetings around lunch time&nbsp;
make the meetings at noon so you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:46.960
to drive in 9 a.m traffic well anytime in l.a&nbsp;
is there's traffic it's all pretty bad yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:53.520
yeah what else did your dad teach you in his final&nbsp;
years I mean I think the things I learned most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.520 --> 00:13:01.280
from him in those final years was that life is not&nbsp;
guaranteed you know tomorrow's not guaranteed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:07.760
seeing him get more and more dehabilitated and&nbsp;
start once the cancer spread to his brain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:12.160
he didn't really understand like who you were all&nbsp;
the time or what exactly was going on all the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:19.840
I think it was really like oh man like you really&nbsp;
need to get your life together and figure out how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:24.560
to accomplish your goals I think in entertainment&nbsp;
people are kind of like oh I want to be this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:28.960
but they never really think about how to be that&nbsp;
they just assume at some point someone calls you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:32.800
and you're like hey I saw this short film you&nbsp;
did you're awesome do you want to direct the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:37.360
next avengers movie which of course would be&nbsp;
amazing but it doesn't really work that way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:42.880
so you got to try to figure out how to get to&nbsp;
the point that you want and everyone's points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:47.200
different I mean some people want to go and&nbsp;
be a makeup artist on a certain television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:52.640
show or somebody wants to go and design props for&nbsp;
something else and there's different ways to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:57.760
everything there's no there's no right way to do&nbsp;
anything in the entertainment industry but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:04.400
are consistent ways that people have been become&nbsp;
successful so I think it's really I think one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:09.520
the things that i've learned that those last few&nbsp;
years was to really laser focus like exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:14.640
I wanted and then figure out the combination&nbsp;
to be able to get to there as fast as I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.640
he and I didn't always see eye to eye he was&nbsp;
definitely a person that didn't understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:30.720
a lot like he wanted me to play football I&nbsp;
can't play football I can't I'm like the least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:35.360
athletic person ever but he played football&nbsp;
so it's like he wanted me to play football&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.760
though he wanted to be a writer and&nbsp;
his parents didn't want to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:43.840
so he had to do what his parents wanted&nbsp;
and luckily he didn't force that on me but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:50.560
it was just kind of a a different&nbsp;
mentality so you always kind of have that

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.920
besides the creature living in&nbsp;
your back you have this little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:59.360
nagging thing that always wishes that&nbsp;
you had a little bit more time with him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.480
that you could have done something that he would&nbsp;
have been impressed by and for me my specific one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:13.520
was he was always obsessed with France with Paris&nbsp;
he loves France he always he went there in college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:20.160
like backpacking like he always want to go back&nbsp;
to France and then three months after he died our&nbsp;&nbsp;

